course: dictionary
lesson: 457
title: A word for a big fleet
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An armada is a large group of warships. In history books you will meet the Spanish Armada, the great fleet that King Philip II of Spain sent to invade England in the time of Queen Elizabeth I. «Elizabeth I ruled England for 45 years, and in 1588 her navy and stormy weather defeated the Spanish Armada.» The word is a clue: a fleet of many ships, sent into battle.
explain: When you meet a new word, look at the words around it. «A storm could wreck a fleet, as it helped wreck the Spanish Armada in 1588.» Fleet and armada are close in meaning here, and wreck tells you the ships were badly damaged. Try the test: swap in "a large group of warships" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Armada can also be used in everyday writing, in an informal way. «An armada of boats filled the harbour» means a very large number of boats, not only warships. So the same word can stretch from a history lesson to a summer day at the seaside, and the context tells you which meaning the writer has in mind.
fact: An armada is a large group of warships.
fact: The Spanish Armada was sent by King Philip II of Spain to invade England in the time of Queen Elizabeth I.
fact: The Spanish Armada was defeated and wrecked by storms, and «1588» is the year given for this in our sentences.
fact: Informally, an armada can mean any large group of boats or vehicles.
fact: Armada is a noun, and its plural is armadas.
exercise: «Elizabeth I's navy and stormy weather defeated the Spanish Armada.» What is an armada? | *A large group of warships | A tall castle on a cliff | A small rowing boat | A stormy sea
exercise: Dad says, «There is an armada of cars on the motorway this morning.» What does armada mean here? | A broken-down lorry | A single fast car | A road closed for repairs | *A very large number of vehicles
exercise: «The Spanish Armada was sent to invade England.» Who sent it? | Francis Drake's crew | *King Philip II of Spain | Queen Elizabeth I | The people of London
test: «A storm could wreck a fleet, as it helped wreck the Spanish Armada in 1588.» What does fleet mean in this sentence? | A large castle | A quiet lake | *A group of ships | A kind of storm
test: What happened to the Spanish Armada? | *It was defeated and wrecked by storms | It was never sent to sea | It won every battle | It sailed home to Spain without a fight
exam: Tom says, «The harbour was an armada of little boats.» What does he mean? | Boats that could not float | A warship with big guns | *Lots of boats, all together | One boat, very big
